```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a missing RIP network statement on router6. Router6's RIP
configuration contains only "network 10.0.0.0/8", which activates solely
eth2 (10.50.1.2/24 — the point-to-point link to router3). The two interfaces
connecting router6 to mswitch2 (Location C) — eth0 at 172.18.50.254/24 and
eth1 at 172.18.51.254/24 — are entirely excluded from the RIP process.

Because router6 participates in RIP on eth2 alone, the only connected prefix
it could include in a periodic Response is 10.50.1.0/24. FRR's ripd suppresses
advertising a subnet back on the interface that subnet belongs to, and
split-horizon removes all routes learned from eth2. The result is that router6
has no route entries to place in a Response packet, so it sends none. Instead,
ripd falls back to transmitting a general RIPv2 Request (the "send me
everything" startup packet) approximately every ten minutes. Router3 marks
router6 as a live peer only when one of these Requests arrives; after 180
seconds with no further traffic, the peer entry times out and is removed.
This is the "bouncing RIP adjacency" the team observed — confirmed by direct
tcpdump evidence showing only Requests, never Responses, from 10.50.1.2, and
by router3's routing-information-sources table for 10.50.1.2 repeatedly
appearing and disappearing on a ~3–10 minute cycle.

Because 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 are never advertised into RIP, no
router on the Location-A-to-Location-C path carries a RIP route to those
subnets, causing the intermittent reachability reported as "flapping."

Root cause: router6 is missing "network 172.18.0.0/16" in its RIP
configuration.

Fix: on router6, enter vtysh and add:
  router rip
   network 172.18.0.0/16
   version 2
This activates eth0 and eth1 in RIP, causes Location C subnets to be
advertised to router3 in every periodic Response, stabilises the adjacency,
and allows Location A ↔ Location C reachability via RIP.
=== END ===
